<main><article class="editorial-story"><header class="story-hero"><p class="section-label">New York · 1896</p><h1>142 hours<br/><em>on the boards.</em></h1><p>Teddy Hale won a six-day race in which every rider was alone, sleep came in short fragments and exhaustion made the track itself difficult to recognise.</p></header>
<figure class="story-image hero portrait"><OptimizedImage src={hero} alt="Teddy Hale riding a track bicycle" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>Teddy Hale, winner of the 1896 six-day race in New York. Contemporary print supplied by the user; public domain.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="story-copy"><p class="lead">When the race ended on Saturday evening, 12 December 1896, Teddy Hale had covered 1,910 miles and eight laps: roughly 3,074 kilometres.</p>
<h2>One rider, six days</h2><p>The early six-day was not yet the pairs event later known as the Madison. In 1896 the riders entered individually. They could stop and sleep, but the clock continued and every pause allowed rivals to gain miles.</p><p>The race at Madison Square Garden began shortly after midnight on Monday and ended at ten on Saturday night, 142 hours later. A contemporary account records 27 starters and 15 finishers. Hale won by a little over 28 miles and beat the previous record by more than 300 miles.</p>
<figure class="story-image inset"><OptimizedImage src={arena} alt="A large indoor cycle track and crowd at Madison Square Garden" widths={[640,960,1400]} sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 1000px"/><figcaption>Madison Square Garden during a six-day race in the early twentieth century. The image shows the later arena around the event Hale helped make famous. Bain News Service/Library of Congress; no known restrictions.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>When sleep disappeared</h2><p>Hale and his nearest rivals had to balance speed against rest. Contemporary newspapers described riders becoming disoriented and losing their senses through fatigue and lack of sleep. Later retellings added vivid visions of snakes, walls and threatening objects among the crowd. Those images belong to the race's folklore, but the surviving sources do not securely attach each incident to Hale.</p><p>The established facts are extreme enough. The riders competed day and night under artificial light, noise and tobacco smoke. Whenever they lay down, they knew the race continued without them.</p>
<h2>The track bicycle offered no rest</h2><p>Track bicycles of the period used a fixed wheel. Whenever the rear wheel turned, the pedals turned with it. A rider could not coast and rest his legs through a bend as on a freewheel bicycle. The mechanism was simple and dependable, but over six days it was also relentless.</p><p>Attendants inside the track supplied food, drink, massage and whatever the period considered stimulating. Coffee, alcohol and various stimulants formed part of late nineteenth-century racing culture. Cocaine, nitroglycerine and strychnine appear in later accounts of the era, but there is no sound basis for stating exactly what Hale received during the 1896 race.</p>
<h2>From individual endurance to the Madison</h2><p>The extreme events drew criticism. Before the 1899 race, New York imposed a limit of 12 hours' riding per rider per day. Promoters answered by pairing riders, allowing the team to remain in the race while the partners took turns to rest.</p><p>That was the beginning of the team form named after Madison Square Garden. Hale had won the older version: six days as an individual. After him, six-day racing increasingly became a matter of partnership, relief and the hand sling that would become the discipline's signature.</p></div>
<figure class="story-image closing"><OptimizedImage src={lineup} alt="A line of six-day riders waiting on an indoor wooden track" widths={[640,960,1200]} sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 900px"/><figcaption>Six-day riders at a later edition of the race. The photograph shows the team era that followed the individual marathons. Historical photograph supplied by the user; public domain.</figcaption></figure>
